            <ArticleTitle>Perceptions of pediatric asthma in African American underserved families using metaphorical analysis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>342-53</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/jspn.12045</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The purpose was to understand how African American children with asthma and their families living in underserved urban areas experience and perceive asthma.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A narrative inquiry design with metaphorical analysis was used in this study with 20 African American children and their 10 families.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Participants metaphorically viewed the family system as a functioning healthcare facility. Children didn't find their asthma abnormal but did use asthma symptoms to their advantage. Caregivers were viewed as first-line medical responders but often made errors in delivering medical treatment. Lack of knowledge relating to appropriate asthma interventions led to insufficient asthma care.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PRACTICE IMPLICATIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Results provide evidence to improve patient-nurse interactions for this vulnerable patient group.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2013, Wiley Periodicals, Inc.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
